.Sh HISTORY
It replaced a self-signed certificate per service, which raced each other
through 1.0.5.
Its directory moved from
.Pa /var/lib/kldload/ca
to
.Pa /etc/kldload/ca
when the dataset mounted over
.Pa /var/lib/kldload
at first boot was found hiding the root the installer had made, so first
boot made a second one that did not match the enrolled key.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page:
.Cm init Fl -force
left the new root's directory readable by root alone, so the root could no
longer be downloaded from the console; the header claimed a Kubernetes
authority and a distribution to virtual machines by Ansible, neither of
which exists; a password set in
.Ev KLDLOAD_MOK_PASSWORD
was written to the log; and a missing command printed the usage and
exited 0.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
One key is both the root of every TLS certificate and the Secure Boot
signing key: whoever has
.Pa ca.key
can both impersonate the machine's services and sign a kernel module it
will load.
.Xr kldload-secure-boot 8
keeps a second copy of it, for DKMS, at
.Pa /var/lib/dkms/mok.key .
.Pp
Nothing removes an issued certificate: a virtual machine that is destroyed
leaves its key in
.Pa leaves/ ,
and
.Cm info
lists it until it is deleted by hand.
There is no revocation.
.Pp
.Cm sign-module
exits 0 when the kernel's
.Pa sign-file
is not found, leaving the module unsigned; with Secure Boot on, the
module then will not load.
.Pp
A new root is not trusted anywhere until
.Cm trust-install
runs again, and a new root is not enrolled for Secure Boot until
.Xr kldload-secure-boot 8
enrols it; modules signed with it will not load until then.
